  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1..Muggy morning to start our day with temps \ngenerally in the 70s and mo clear to partly cloudy skies across\nthe region. Continue to expect partly cloudy skies and hot and \nhumid conditions today and tomorrow as flow generally remains \nW-SW'rly keeping low level thicknesses around 1430m today and \ntomorrow. Based off our local climatological study, this is \nforecast to result in highs in the mid to upper 90s across the \ninland areas and upper 80s to low 90s along the OBX and coast \neach day with lows in the 70s each night. Combined with \ndewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s, heat indices look to get\naround 105-110F each afternoon. As a result, a Heat Advisory \nremains in effect today for most of ENC. A Heat Advisory will \nlikely be needed Friday as well once the current one expires \nunless the forecast changes substantially. There is a low end \nchance for some extreme heat warning criteria being met mainly \nin our SW'rn zones on Fri, but latest NBM/HREF probs of seeing \nheat indices above 110F on Fri remain low (generally less than \n20%) so not planning on upgrading headlines for now for Fri. We \nencourage anyone planning on spending alot of time outdoors this\nweek to take proper precautions and stay hydrated.\n\nA front impacts the region Fri evening into Sat resulting in \ncooler temperatures this weekend. Temps may briefly warm up once\nagain early next week as well bringing yet another threat of\nheat advisory conditions to ENC next week.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Low precip chances continue today, with one or \ntwo isolated showers/tstms possible along the sea breeze. \nAcross the upper levels, N'rn stream Jet remains active and \ngenerally to the north of the Carolinas with several troughs \ntransiting across it over the next several days. Troughing will\ndig S across the Mid-Atlantic on Fri before pushing offshore\nwith a second trough then tracking across the region early next\nweek. At the mid levels, weak shortwave will transit along the\nperiphery of a ridge centered over the Gulf States today, but\nwith dry air in place and subsidence aloft not expecting much in\nthe way of precip today outside of the seabreeze. Because of \nthis have only kept SChc PoPs along the sea breeze this \nafternoon. An approaching complex of showers/tstms is forecast \nto near our northern zones this evening. However, model trends \nsuggest the best energy remains to our north, and as a result we\nmay avoid all of the activity tonight. However, given low \npredictability, elected to keep Schc PoPs across the N'rn \ncounties for tonight.\n\nAs mentioned above, a stronger trough is progged to push across\nthe Mid-Atlantic Fri with the attendant surface cold front \npushing into the area Fri evening into Sat bringing the best \nchances for showers and storms across ENC. Some of these storms\non Fri into Fri night could become strong to severe in nature \nas they near the area given ample instability. With PWATs also \nforecast to get above 2\" on Fri as well, frequent lightning, \ntorrential rain, and gusty winds will be possible in the \nstrongest storms. SPC currently has the far western coastal \nPlain in a marginal risk for severe weather (level 1/5) for \ngusty winds, while WPC has the entire area under a marginal \nrisk for excessive rainfall (level 1/4) given low end flash\nflooding risk. Precip becomes more isolated in nature by Sat \nafternoon as the front pushes offshore. \n\nHigh pressure briefly builds into the area Sat/Sun bringing \nbringing a drier airmass and minimal chances for storms. \nHowever, a second frontal boundary looks to approach early next\nweek which could once again bring increased chances for showers\nand storms to ENC.",
